WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.967
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online C two, Book three.

2
00:00:05.267 --> 00:00:08.886
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.186 --> 00:00:16.192
Unit three: Behavioral Economics. Today you will explain choices using leverage / exploit + noun phrase.

4
00:00:16.492 --> 00:00:19.074
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.033 --> 00:00:23.057
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.917 --> 00:00:25.382
Cognitive bias.

7
00:00:26.383 --> 00:00:30.867
Cognitive bias means a systematic error in thinking that affects judgment.

8
00:00:31.483 --> 00:00:34.272
Confirmation is a common cognitive bias.

9
00:00:34.883 --> 00:00:38.511
Your turn. Say: Cognitive bias.

10
00:00:41.367 --> 00:00:42.937
Cognitive bias.

11
00:00:43.683 --> 00:00:44.824
Loss aversion.

12
00:00:45.817 --> 00:00:50.173
Loss aversion means the tendency to feel losses more strongly than equal gains.

13
00:00:50.783 --> 00:00:53.944
Loss aversion makes people keep failing investments.

14
00:00:54.550 --> 00:00:57.168
Your turn. Say: Loss aversion.

15
00:01:00.033 --> 00:01:01.429
Loss aversion.

16
00:01:02.183 --> 00:01:03.010
Anchoring.

17
00:01:04.017 --> 00:01:07.282
Anchoring means relying too heavily on the first number seen.

18
00:01:07.883 --> 00:01:11.055
Anchoring on the original price made the discount look generous.

19
00:01:11.650 --> 00:01:14.140
Your turn. Say: Anchoring.

20
00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:18.082
Anchoring.

21
00:01:18.817 --> 00:01:19.667
Nudge.

22
00:01:20.683 --> 00:01:24.703
Nudge means a gentle change in choice design that steers behaviour.

23
00:01:25.317 --> 00:01:28.059
A default enrolment is a powerful nudge.

24
00:01:28.650 --> 00:01:31.082
Your turn. Say: Nudge.

25
00:01:33.933 --> 00:01:34.714
Nudge.

26
00:01:35.450 --> 00:01:37.113
Irrational choice.

27
00:01:38.117 --> 00:01:42.287
Irrational choice means a decision that contradicts one's own stated goals.

28
00:01:42.883 --> 00:01:45.997
Skipping savings for a sale is an irrational choice.

29
00:01:46.583 --> 00:01:50.223
Your turn. Say: Irrational choice.

30
00:01:53.067 --> 00:01:54.648
Irrational choice.

31
00:01:55.383 --> 00:01:56.326
Heuristic.

32
00:01:57.317 --> 00:02:00.233
Heuristic means a mental shortcut for quick judgment.

33
00:02:00.850 --> 00:02:03.744
A familiar brand serves as a heuristic for quality.

34
00:02:04.350 --> 00:02:07.060
Your turn. Say: Heuristic.

35
00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:10.820
Heuristic.

36
00:02:11.583 --> 00:02:12.979
Prospect theory.

37
00:02:13.983 --> 00:02:18.734
Prospect theory means the theory that people judge outcomes relative to a reference point.

38
00:02:19.350 --> 00:02:22.429
Prospect theory won Kahneman a Nobel Prize.

39
00:02:23.017 --> 00:02:26.145
Your turn. Say: Prospect theory.

40
00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:30.361
Prospect theory.

41
00:02:31.117 --> 00:02:32.698
Bounded rationality.

42
00:02:33.683 --> 00:02:38.736
Bounded rationality means reasoning limited by time, information and mental capacity.

43
00:02:39.350 --> 00:02:42.580
Bounded rationality explains why we settle for good enough.

44
00:02:43.183 --> 00:02:46.358
Your turn. Say: Bounded rationality.

45
00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:50.874
Bounded rationality.

46
00:02:51.667 --> 00:02:55.178
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:56.017 --> 00:03:00.210
Visal, why do people buy extra insurance they will almost never use?

48
00:03:01.317 --> 00:03:09.530
Loss aversion. The pain of a possible loss outweighs the pleasure of an equal gain, and sellers exploit that bias.

49
00:03:10.617 --> 00:03:12.221
Is that manipulation?

50
00:03:13.317 --> 00:03:23.481
Not always. A nudge leverages predictable cognitive bias, but it can serve people, for example by making savings the default.

51
00:03:24.583 --> 00:03:26.629
So the same tool can help or harm?

52
00:03:27.717 --> 00:03:35.524
Exactly. Marketers exploit anchoring, whereas governments leverage defaults to improve health and savings.

53
00:03:36.667 --> 00:03:38.388
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:39.250 --> 00:03:43.351
Grammar focus: Leverage and exploit plus noun phrase.

55
00:03:43.651 --> 00:03:44.873
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:45.583 --> 00:03:48.570
A nudge leverages predictable cognitive bias.

57
00:03:49.483 --> 00:03:52.841
Marketers exploit anchoring and loss aversion.

58
00:03:53.750 --> 00:03:56.887
Governments harness default options to improve savings.

59
00:03:57.833 --> 00:04:08.949
Leverage, exploit and harness take a direct object (no preposition): leverages bias, exploits weakness. 'Exploit' can sound negative; 'leverage' and 'harness' are neutral or positive.

60
00:04:09.683 --> 00:04:13.833
Be careful! Don't say: A nudge leverages on bias.

61
00:04:15.800 --> 00:04:18.136
Say: A nudge leverages bias.

62
00:04:18.436 --> 00:04:20.250
leverage takes a direct object.

63
00:04:21.067 --> 00:04:23.440
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:24.233 --> 00:04:25.896
Predictably Irrational.

65
00:04:26.196 --> 00:04:30.796
Behavioural economics tests the assumption that people always choose rationally.

66
00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:38.362
Kahneman and Tversky showed that human judgment relies on heuristics, which produce systematic cognitive biases.

67
00:04:39.367 --> 00:04:45.662
Under prospect theory, loss aversion makes a loss feel roughly twice as painful as an equal gain feels pleasant.

68
00:04:46.667 --> 00:04:51.731
A nudge leverages these regularities, for instance by making retirement saving the default option.

69
00:04:52.733 --> 00:04:57.914
Critics warn that the same techniques can be exploited to manipulate, so transparency is essential.

70
00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:03.118
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:03.983 --> 00:05:06.772
A nudge blank predictable cognitive bias.

72
00:05:07.072 --> 00:05:11.306
A, leverages. B, leverages on. C, leverages of.

73
00:05:14.867 --> 00:05:17.145
The answer is A: leverages.

74
00:05:18.283 --> 00:05:20.852
Feeling losses more than equal gains is blank.

75
00:05:21.152 --> 00:05:24.561
A, anchoring. B, loss aversion. C, nudge.

76
00:05:28.100 --> 00:05:30.494
The answer is B: loss aversion.

77
00:05:31.650 --> 00:05:34.648
Relying too heavily on the first number seen is blank.

78
00:05:34.948 --> 00:05:39.217
A, anchoring. B, heuristic. C, prospect.

79
00:05:42.767 --> 00:05:44.766
The answer is A: anchoring.

80
00:05:45.917 --> 00:05:47.823
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:48.123 --> 00:05:54.970
A, Sellers exploit of bias.. B, Sellers exploit bias.. C, Sellers exploit to bias..

82
00:05:58.533 --> 00:06:01.195
The answer is B: Sellers exploit bias..

83
00:06:02.350 --> 00:06:05.441
According to the reading, critics warn that nudges can be…

84
00:06:05.741 --> 00:06:10.161
A, rational. B, exploited to manipulate. C, useless.

85
00:06:13.700 --> 00:06:16.686
The answer is B: exploited to manipulate.

86
00:06:17.900 --> 00:06:21.284
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:22.150 --> 00:06:25.241
Have you ever noticed loss aversion affecting your own decisions?

88
00:06:42.050 --> 00:06:43.678
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:43.978 --> 00:07:04.736
Yes. I once kept a phone plan I no longer needed because cancelling felt like losing something I had already paid for. That is loss aversion: the fear of losing outweighed the logic of saving money. Now I ask what I would choose if I were starting fresh, which reduces the effect.

90
00:07:05.036 --> 00:07:06.965
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:07.850 --> 00:07:12.720
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and leverage and exploit plus noun phrase.

92
00:07:13.020 --> 00:07:17.286
Today's value is self-reflection. Look honestly at your own choices.

93
00:07:17.586 --> 00:07:20.087
See you in Unit four. Goodbye!

94
00:07:20.387 --> 00:07:22.889
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

